---
.title = "The Smart Zone Is a Property of Your Codebase",
.date = .date("2026-11-05T00:00:00"),
.authors = ["Ben O'Mahony"],
.layout = "post.shtml",
.draft = true,
---

Matt Pocock keeps talking about the smart zone.
The framing comes from Dex Horthy at HumanLayer.
The idea is simple.
A model reasons well up to a certain amount of context.
Past that point it gets worse.
Matt puts the line at roughly 150k tokens.

The observation is real, but the causality is inverted. 

## A number without a codebase

Treating 150k as a universal threshold assumes the cost of orientation is fixed.
It is not.
The tokens an agent consumes before it can make a useful change depend on what it has to read.
What it has to read depends on the codebase.

So a smart zone of 150k tokens tells you something.
It tells you about the codebases it was measured on.
It does not tell you about models in general.

## An accidental measurement

Giles Edwards-Alexander published [an experiment on the economic benefit of refactoring](https://martinfowler.com/articles/exploring-gen-ai/refactoring-economic-benefit.html).
He built a substantial application entirely with agents.
Around 150,000 lines, mostly Rust.
Nobody reviewed the code.
The data access layer grew into a single file of 17,155 lines.

He then refactored that file in small, strictly behaviour preserving steps.
After each step he gave a fresh subagent exactly the same feature request and measured how many tokens it consumed.
Fresh agents do not learn, so each run is comparable.

The baseline change consumed 159,564 input tokens.
After the final step the same change consumed 27,360.
That is an 83% reduction.

Look at those two numbers again.
The baseline sits almost exactly on Matt's threshold.
The refactored codebase lands around 25k.
Same model.
Same task.
Same accompanying documentation.
The only thing that moved was the structure of the code.

The total amount of code barely changed.
The data access layer stayed at roughly 16,500 lines throughout.
The saving came from the agent being able to find the smallest set of files it needed and read only those.
Giles notes that splitting the file at random would probably not have helped.
The earlier steps removed duplication and named the repeating core.
Only then did the split pay off.

## Comprehension cost

Here is the inversion.
The smart zone threshold is not a constant of the model.
It is a measure of how well intent is compressed into the code.

Call it comprehension cost.
The number of tokens an agent must consume before it can make a correct change.
A codebase with clear module boundaries, a ubiquitous language, precise types and tests that state intent has a low comprehension cost.
A tangled one has a high cost.

This turns a folk threshold into a score.
You can track it.
You can watch it go down when you refactor and up when you let things rot.

## Markdown is expansion, not compression

The common response to high comprehension cost is to write more markdown.
AGENTS.md.
Architecture notes.
Module summaries.
People believe they are compressing understanding for the agent.

They are doing the opposite.
A summary is lossy.
It drops precision to save space.
So the agent reads the summary, learns roughly where to look, and then reads the code anyway to find out what is actually true.
Total context goes up, not down.

It gets worse with time.
Code is checked by the compiler and the tests.
Prose is checked by nobody.
The summary drifts.
A drifted summary is not merely useless.
It is actively misleading, and the agent has no way to know.

This part is argument, not measurement.
Giles held the documentation constant.
His experiment says nothing directly about whether the markdown helped or hurt.
It does show that structure alone moved the cost by a factor of six.

## Grep beats prose

If the aim is retrieval, the best index you have is precise names in the code.
Grep is exact.
It is cheap.
It is never stale.

A markdown file describing where things live is a slower and less reliable search index layered over the real one.
Every hour spent writing it would be better spent renaming things so that the search works.

Good naming is search engine optimisation for agents.

## Where intent should live

Put intent where tooling can check it.

* Names that carry domain meaning.
* Types that make illegal states unrepresentable.
* Module boundaries that follow bounded contexts.
* Tests that state what the system must do.

Each of these is both documentation and constraint.
Each is verified on every build.

## What prose is still for

Code is poor at one thing.
It cannot easily record why an alternative was rejected.
Decision records and the reasons behind awkward constraints can earn their tokens.

The test is simple.
Does the prose carry information the code cannot?
If it only restates the code, delete it.

## What the evidence does and does not show

I want to be careful here.

Giles ran one experiment.
One representative change.
One greenfield codebase with a single developer.
Rust.
Token counts were approximated from character counts.
The curve was not monotonic.
Two middle steps made the change more expensive than the baseline.
Output tokens barely moved at all.
A result in Rust is not evidence about TypeScript or Python.

The refactoring was not free either.
Giles puts the upper bound at five million tokens, and that bound includes other work.
At that bound, the saving pays back after fewer than forty changes to the same area.
Whether that is a good deal depends on how often you touch the code.

So the experiment supports the direction of my claim.
It does not establish its size or its generality.

## An experiment worth running

Here is what I would like to see.

Take a fixed set of tasks.
Run them against several codebases of differing structural quality.
Measure tokens consumed before the first correct change.
Judge correctness with binary pass or fail checks, never with a model asked for a score.
Then run every task again with the markdown layer removed.

Two results would falsify the thesis.
If comprehension cost barely moves with structural quality, the universal smart zone wins.
If removing the markdown consistently raises the cost, my claim about expansion is wrong.

## The ceiling is still there

None of this says context degradation vanishes.
Long context effects are real and documented.
The ceiling exists.

The claim is that the floor moves.
A well structured codebase spends less of the budget on orientation and more on the task.
It stays further from the ceiling for longer.

## Earn the smart zone

If your agent needs 150k tokens before it does anything useful, that number is feedback about your code.
Remove duplication.
Name the core.
Tighten the types.
Draw the boundaries where the domain draws them.
Then delete the markdown that only restates the code.

The smart zone is not given to you by the model.
You earn it.
